Z||000000||fldevel||04-23-2001||10:40 AM|||| i saw a lil bottle at the local auto parts shop that sed NOS on it .. upon further inspection you just add it to your fuel tank .. has anyone ever tried this stuff?? any HP increases exc..||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-23-2001||10:50 AM||||Sounds just like a marked up fuel system cleaner/octane booster.  I tried 104+ and noticed nothing.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Fiero87311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto near completion!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||04-23-2001||11:02 AM||||If you read the label, it says it's octane boost.  Has nothing to do with Nitrous.  I tried it in my '99 Grand Am 3400, and it did seems to run smoother and a bit snappier, but not much faster.  I doubt a stock Fiero would notice much difference.||||reg||6
Z||000003||1FST2M6||04-23-2001||11:09 AM||||it's been dyno proven that additives don't make you faster.. additives and nitrous, timing changes, more boost the fuels abilty to be more stable at higher temps is why your car CAN be faster with other mods. but just adding the octane to an other wise stock car is just tossing your money away.. most fuel system cleaners  (according to what we have found) are also just a waste of time.. the fuel in most of todays tanks have high levels of cleaners and system conditioners. and most of us being spirited drivers (driving a Fiero like it should be driven -balls to the wall-) will clean most of the carbon deposits out of any valve seats or injector boss. the carbon or oil build up in the intake can just be clean out with 30 min and a some carb cleaner... (V6) take it off drench it and re-assemble it. but inless you have Nitrous or forced induction you won't see any increase in performace. Hot Rod magazine did a study with 87-89-93 and 110 the 87 made more hp in a stock motor than the 110 did but the 110 made more hp in the higher compression motor.. where the 87 octane in the same HI_PO motor nearly caused engine failure.   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jimmy||04-23-2001||01:42 PM||||I am always explaining this to friends of mine.  Especially the import guys.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Its so simple its silly.  Like 1fst2m6 was saying, The lowest possible octane for a four stroke motor with out ping or detonation is going to give you the most power/best fuel economy.  Higher octane has/are  retardants in it to slow/delay the combustion for higher compression motors.  87 octane gas is more powerfull than 100 octane in theory.<P>We all know this, but how many times have you heard some guy with an Integra(stock motor) say "Its the weekend so I'm gonna go juice up with some race gas so I can go whup on some Neons!" I nearly LMAO every time I hear this!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I have even seen stickers that say what octane they(rice boys) are running in their car as if its some sort of performance mod. hehe<P>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000005||Voytek||04-23-2001||02:23 PM||||Not to contradict everything said so far, but I DO notice a performance difference with higher octane gas.  My 2.8 stock (now bored out to 3.1) ALWAYS seemed to have more power with higher octane.  I normally run 87 octane gas but every now and then treat myself (that's right, treat myself - it's very pricy up here) to some Mohawk 94 octane (although now they call it a 92). Perhaps the smootheness of the engine allows for faster acceleration but whatever it is, it does seem to work for power.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||04-23-2001||03:21 PM||||Voytek, (others correct me if I am wrong) but since your 3.1 is a stroked 2.8 your compression ratio is higher and therefore may indeed benfit from a higher octane gasoline. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jimmy||04-23-2001||04:35 PM||||I'm not sure how controlling(i imagine very little) the Fiero ECM is to todays standards, but most cars' computers will adjust the timing for different octanes if you use the wrong one.  For example: T-bird Supercoupes take Premium 91 gas put you can run Standard 87 gas in emergencies at the expense of performance.  The computer will retard the timing I believe.<P>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cameron||04-23-2001||06:06 PM||||if it did work then you could literally open a can of whoop a**.  That could be funny.  But it doesn't suprise me in the slightest that it is just an octane boost.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||FLASHY||04-25-2001||11:44 AM||||I could be wrong, but I think Voytek was saying higher octane gas has always given his car a little "boost" even when it was stock 2.8<P>I for one agree with him (if that's what he's saying). Regardless of the logic, I know I feel a bit more punch with higher octane gas (I have stock 2.8) and it does seem to run smoother. I (like Voytek said) run most of the time on 87 so I'm used to it, and when I "treat myself" to 92 I can feel the difference. Mind you it's not a huge difference but none the less there's something.||||reg||1
